Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be even functions. We want to determine if $f(x)g(x)$ is even, odd, or neither. 
Recall that a function $h(x)$ is even if $h(-x) = h(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $h$. 
A function $h(x)$ is odd if $h(-x) = -h(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $h$. 
Since $f(x)$ is even, $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x$. 
Since $g(x)$ is even, $g(-x) = g(x)$ for all $x$. 
Then $f(x)g(x)$ is defined as $(f(x)g(x))(-x) = f(x)g(-x)$. 
Because $g(x)$ is even, $g(-x) = g(x)$. 
Therefore, $f(x)g(-x) = f(x)g(x)$. 
However, we want to know if $f(x)g(x)$ is even, odd, or neither. We have 
$f(x)g(x) = f(x)g(-x)$. 
If $f(x)g(x) = f(-x)g(-x)$, then $f(x)g(x) = f(-x)g(-x) = f(x)g(x)$. 
We know $f(x)$ is even and $g(x)$ is even. 
We have $f(x) g(x) = f(-x) g(-x)$.
If $f(x)=x^2$ and $g(x)=x^2$, then $f(x)g(x) = x^4$, which is even.
If $f(x)=x^2$ and $g(x)=-x^2$, then $f(x) g(x) = -x^4$, which is odd.
If $f(x) g(x)$ is even, then $f(x) g(x) = f(-x) g(-x)$.
Since $f(x)$ is even, $f(-x) = f(x)$. 
Since $g(x)$ is even, $g(-x) = g(x)$. 
Therefore, we have $f(x) g(x) = f(x) g(x)$, which is always true. 
Thus, $f(x)g(x)$ is even.

For $f(x) g(x)$ to be even, we need $f(-x) g(-x) = f(x) g(x)$. 
Since $f(x)$ is even, $f(-x) = f(x)$. 
Since $g(x)$ is even, $g(-x) = g(x)$. 
Therefore, $f(-x) g(-x) = f(x) g(x)$. 
So $f(x) g(x)$ is even. 

