Let the equation be $z^6 + z^3 + 1 = 0$. We can make a substitution $w = z^3$. Then the equation becomes $w^2 + w + 1 = 0$. Using the quadratic formula, we have:
$$ w = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \frac{-1 \pm i\sqrt{3}}{2} $$
So $w = e^{i(2\pi/3)}$ and $w = e^{i(4\pi/3)}$.
Since $z^3 = w$, we have $z = \sqrt[3]{w}$. We want to find the complex roots of $z^6 + z^3 + 1 = 0$.
For $z^3 = e^{i(2\pi/3)}$, we have $z = \sqrt[3]{e^{i(2\pi/3)}} = e^{i(2\pi/9 + 2\pi k/3)}$, where $k = 0, 1, 2$.
$$ z_0 = e^{i(2\pi/9)} \\ z_1 = e^{i(2\pi/9 + 2\pi/3)} = e^{i(8\pi/9)} \\ z_2 = e^{i(2\pi/9 + 4\pi/3)} = e^{i(14\pi/9)} $$
For $z^3 = e^{i(4\pi/3)}$, we have $z = \sqrt[3]{e^{i(4\pi/3)}} = e^{i(4\pi/9 + 2\pi k/3)}$, where $k = 0, 1, 2$.
$$ z_3 = e^{i(4\pi/9)} \\ z_4 = e^{i(4\pi/9 + 2\pi/3)} = e^{i(10\pi/9)} \\ z_5 = e^{i(4\pi/9 + 4\pi/3)} = e^{i(16\pi/9)} $$
We are looking for a root with argument $\theta$ between $90^\circ$ and $180^\circ$.
$z_0 = e^{i(2\pi/9)} \approx e^{i(34.37^\circ)}$
$z_1 = e^{i(8\pi/9)} \approx e^{i(160.32^\circ)}$
$z_2 = e^{i(14\pi/9)} \approx e^{i(234.42^\circ)}$
$z_3 = e^{i(4\pi/9)} \approx e^{i(84.29^\circ)}$
$z_4 = e^{i(10\pi/9)} \approx e^{i(209.44^\circ)}$
$z_5 = e^{i(16\pi/9)} \approx e^{i(282.48^\circ)}$
Only $z_1$ has an argument between $90^\circ$ and $180^\circ$.
Therefore, $\theta = \frac{8\pi}{9} \approx 160.32^\circ$.
